# Triaged gitleaks findings in git HISTORY, as `<rule>:<secret>` pairs.
#
# The history scan cannot use .gitleaksignore: a fingerprint is
# <file>:<rule>:<line>, and the same content appears at many different line
# numbers across history, so fingerprints do not converge. This file records
# the distinct finding set instead, and `just secrets` fails if the scan
# produces any pair not listed here. New history findings therefore fail the
# gate; known ones do not.
#
# Every entry below is either a Figma document identifier — a file key or a
# 40-hex component/style key, neither of which grants access on its own — or
# one of Figma's two expired presigned-URL AWS key IDs, which are recorded
# with their full reasoning in .secrets-triaged.
#
# Triaged 2026-08-10. Regenerate with:
#   gitleaks git --log-opts="--all" --config .gitleaks.toml \
#     --report-format json --report-path /tmp/h.json
#   jq -r '.[] | "\(.RuleID):\(.Secret)"' /tmp/h.json | sort -u
#
# Adding a line is a claim the value was read and understood.
